# Copy to .env before starting.

# ── This copy of the stack ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Prefixes the Compose project, the network and the data volume. Leave it alone
# unless you want a second, independent archive on the same machine - then give
# that copy its own value here AND in every other component's .env, and the
# two never touch each other's containers, network or data.
#
# Two copies sharing this name share the volume, and `docker compose down` in
# one of them pulls the network out from under the other - which looks like a
# database that runs, reports healthy, and refuses every connection.
ARCHIVE_STACK=xtracting

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Change this. The archive holds your extraction results.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

POSTGRES_DB=xtracting_archive
POSTGRES_USER=xtracting

# Host port. Bound to 127.0.0.1 by the compose file, so nothing is exposed to
# the network. Change it if 5432 is already taken by a local PostgreSQL.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# ── Application roles (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave all four empty and the dashboard and the crawler connect as
# POSTGRES_USER, the owner. That is fine on a machine only you reach.
#
# Set all four and init/04-roles.sh creates two further logins on first start:
# the dashboard's, which can read everything but write only its own tables
# and the crawler configuration, and the crawler's, which can read the
# configuration but write only its own state and run log. Neither can change
# a row of the archive. Put the same values into ../dashboard/standard/.env and
# ../crawler/.env as POSTGRES_USER / POSTGRES_PASSWORD there.
#
# Two of the four set and two empty stops the container from starting, on
# purpose - half a configuration is a typo. To add the roles to an archive
# that already exists, run the script by hand; docs/USAGE.md shows how.
DASHBOARD_DB_USER=
DASHBOARD_DB_PASSWORD=
SCRAPER_DB_USER=
SCRAPER_DB_PASSWORD=
